{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-109582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}-10y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}+10y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}+10{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10{x}_{1}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}"] ["math" "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}"] ["math" "10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+3{t}^{1/2}{y}^{″}-7y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{t}^{1/2}{y}^{″}+7y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}+7{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=7{x}_{1}-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=7{x}_{1}-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}"] ["math" "-7{x}_{2}+3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}"] ["math" "3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{1}-7{x}_{3}"] ["math" "4{t}^{3/2}{x}_{1}+3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}"] ["math" "7{x}_{1}-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7{x}_{1}-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}+4ty=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}-4ty.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}-4t{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}"] ["math" "-4{e}^{t}{x}_{1}+4t{x}_{2}"] ["math" "4t{x}_{1}-4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}"] ["math" "4{e}^{t}{x}_{1}+4t{x}_{2}"] ["math" "4{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-4t{x}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{″}-{t}^{3}{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{″}+{t}^{3}{y}^{′}-2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-2{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=-2{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}"] ["math" "2{x}_{1}-{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}"] ["math" "{t}^{3}{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}-2\\,{x}_{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+{e}^{t}{y}^{″}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{e}^{t}{y}^{″}+{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}+{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)={e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t{x}_{1}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{2}+{e}^{t}{x}_{3}"] ["math" "{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}"] ["math" "{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{2}-t{x}_{3}"] ["math" "{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{2}+t{x}_{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-6\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(1)=-3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(1)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate  $(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=2 \\\\ {y}^{″}(1) & ={x}_{3}(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=6\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}-12{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=2 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-2,q=2,r=-3,f(t)=-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(-2+2-(-3))(-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(-2+2+3)(-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =3(-6)(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-18(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-18(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3})"] ["math" "-4(6{x}_{2}-3\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3})"] ["math" "-6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}"] ["math" "-8(6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3})"] ["math" "6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-18(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-ty=0,\\,y(2)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(2)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(2)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & ={x}_{1}(2)=-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(2) & ={x}_{2}(2)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-4{y}^{′}+ty.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-4{x}_{2}+t{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(2)=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(2)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-2,q=-3,f(t)=t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(-2-(-3))(t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =(1)(t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2(4{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2})"] ["math" "-t{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}"] ["math" "2(t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2})"] ["math" "3(4{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2})"] ["math" "t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+3{y}^{′}=0,\\,y(2)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1,\\,{y}^{″}(2)=2\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(2)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(2)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(2)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate $(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & ={x}_{1}(2)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(2) & ={x}_{2}(2)=1 \\\\ {y}^{″}(2) & ={x}_{3}(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(2)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(2)=1 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-3{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{3}(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-1,q=1,r=2,f(t)=-3{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(-1+1-2)(-3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =(-2)(-3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =6{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12{x}_{1}"] ["math" "-15{x}_{3}"] ["math" "-9{x}_{2}"] ["math" "21{x}_{1}"] ["math" "6{x}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6{x}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-107906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-107906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}-2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=3,f(t)=-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-3)f(t) \\\\  & =-f(t) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-107906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-107906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-107906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2(2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2})"] ["math" "-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}"] ["math" "2(2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2})"] ["math" "2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}"] ["math" "4(2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-107906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+3{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}-12y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q+r)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=1 \\\\ {y}^{″}(0) & ={x}_{3}(0)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+12y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=1 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=1,r=3,f(t)=12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q+r)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-1+3)(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =4(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})"] ["math" "4(-12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})"] ["math" "4(-3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+12{x}_{3})"] ["math" "4(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})"] ["math" "5(3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}+y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{1}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{3} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{3} & -3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+5{y}^{″}-3{t}^{2}{y}^{′}=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & b \\\\ 0 & a & c\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-5{y}^{″}+3{t}^{2}{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-5{x}_{3}+3{t}^{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=3{t}^{2}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3{t}^{2} & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=3{t}^{2},b=1,c=-5,$ and $a+b+c=3{t}^{2}-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-(3{t}^{2}+2)"] ["math" "1-3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}+1"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{t}^{2}-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-2{y}^{″}+13{y}^{′}=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & b & c\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=2{y}^{″}-13{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=2{x}_{3}-13{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-13{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=1,b=-13,c=2,$ and $a+b+c=-10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10"] ["math" "-12"] ["math" "-14"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "14"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-3{y}^{′}+4y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ a & c & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=3{y}^{′}-4y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=-4,b=1,c=3,$ and $a+b+c=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+3y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{y}^{′}-3y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c782ee2399b6a9b97f193baedba84672babeb0829507a9969172126d64f53c4"} {"question" "q-109646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "q-109646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee43de708d6d9f76443dc0578e875535b87b383ef0437c50d5022d65a68066da"} {"question" "e-9040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\ny''' - 3\\sqrt{t}\\, y'' + t^3 y' - 4t^2 y= 0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t),$ $x_2(t) = y'(t)$ and $x_3(t) = y''(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/example-9040.json" "file_sha256" "5aef6bed8ed4cbda84604cca53e4476574a283801d82e73f0c2a03fee21687ef"} {"question" "e-9040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2,$ and $x_3.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\,y'' - t^3 y' + 4t^2 y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\, x_3 - t^3 x_2 + 4t^2 x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=4{t}^{2}\\,{x}_{1}-{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+3\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = 4t^2 x_1 - t^3 x_2 + 3\\sqrt{t} \\,x_3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/example-9040.json" "file_sha256" "5aef6bed8ed4cbda84604cca53e4476574a283801d82e73f0c2a03fee21687ef"} {"question" "e-9041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny''  + y' - 6y= 0 , \\quad y(0)= 1, \\quad y'(0)= 4\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t),$ $x_2(t) = y'(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/example-9041.json" "file_sha256" "2bea0829ab04024d1e3333f107c8fd20d28c599ce9311899c4e455daa38dbcbc"} {"question" "e-9041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = - y' + 6y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = - x_2 + 6x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=1, q=4, f(t) = 6x_1 - x_2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(1-4)(6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3(6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/example-9041.json" "file_sha256" "2bea0829ab04024d1e3333f107c8fd20d28c599ce9311899c4e455daa38dbcbc"} {"question" "e-9138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order differential equation $y'' + 5y' - 2y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/59e386be8b30fca475a2e0d164687a6561b9ab368b9879fc301d328896d184fd.png)\nFind the matrix $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/example-9138.json" "file_sha256" "7a436d2cc528bd2b8372230e542d5e17a1cc6694d7835eebdde5caa345949eca"} {"question" "e-9138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''= -5y' + 2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = -5x_2 + 2x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=2{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/f35de5e6f066144c4130e38304abbff552730dc1fe97828572a66e68970c5d52.png)\n\nHence, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/314f9dcf277b61dac9985c448aed98113516592e3822cc47a8566d28a480651b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/example-9138.json" "file_sha256" "7a436d2cc528bd2b8372230e542d5e17a1cc6694d7835eebdde5caa345949eca"}] "usage" {"q-107906" [] "e-9040" [] "e-9041" [] "e-9138" []} "basis" 2476 "retractions" [[17592186166995 :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n$y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = 0, y(1) = 2, y^{′}(1) = 3$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(1) = p \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = f(t),\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(1) = q$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate $(p - q) \\cdot f(t)$."] [17592186166632 :question/problem "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y''' - 3\\sqrt{t}\\, y'' + t^3 y' - 4t^2 y= 0$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = f(t)$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$, $x_2(t) = y'(t)$ and $x_3(t) = y''(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$."] [17592186166632 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{3} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\nx_{1}^{′} = y^{′}; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3},; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$, and $x_3$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\,y'' - t^3 y' + 4t^2 y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\, x_3 - t^3 x_2 + 4t^2 x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = 4t^{2}x_{1} - t^{3}x_{2} + 3\\sqrt{t}x_{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = 4t^2 x_1 - t^3 x_2 + 3\\sqrt{t} \\,x_3\n$$"] [17592186166633 :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n$y'' + y' - 6y= 0, \\quad y(0)= 1, \\quad y'(0)= 4$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(0) = p \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = f(t),\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(0) = q$\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$, $x_2(t) = y'(t)$, and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate $(p-q)\\cdot f(t)$."] [17592186166633 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= y \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= x_{1}(0) = 1 \\\\\ny^{′}(0) &= x_{2}(0) = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′}(t) &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′}(t) &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' =-y' + 6y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' =-x_2 + 6x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(0) = 1 \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = 6x_{1} - x_{2},\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(0) = 4\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np=1, q=4, f(t) = 6x_1 - x_2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(p - q) \\cdot f(t) &= (1 - 4)(6x_{1} - x_{2}) \\\\\n&=-3(6x_{1} - x_{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166636 :question/problem "The second-order differential equation $y'' + 5y' - 2y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the matrix $A$."] [17592186166636 :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''= -5y' + 2y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = -5x_2 + 2x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = 2x_{1} - 5x_{2}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′} \\\\ x_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163389 17592186163390 17592186163391 17592186163392 17592186163393] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163388 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163388 :answer-field/id] [17592186163388 :answer-field/key] [17592186163388 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
